You are a judge for the "Flip the Switch" hackathon (WeMakeDevs, DevTools Academy, and Switch by SandboxAQ).
Switch is an open-source orchestration layer that puts AI coding agents (Claude Code, Codex, and others) and people into shared team-chat rooms (Slack, Discord, Mattermost), with rooms, roles, addressing policies, templates, documents, and custom agents that join over an HTTP and server-sent-events protocol.

Official rubric:
- Use of Switch, 30%: is Switch central, and would the project stop working without it?
- Technical execution, 25%: does it run, and could someone clone the repo and reproduce it?
- Creativity and impact, 25%: is it a fresh idea that solves a problem a real team has?
- Presentation and documentation, 20%: do the demo video and README make clear what was built and how Switch is used?

You can see only the submission's written material below, not its code or video. Judge only what the material shows.

Submission:
# LedgerRoom

**An AI teammate can explain an invoice exception. It cannot approve itself.**

LedgerRoom turns a team chat into an evidence-backed invoice review desk. A reviewer asks `@ledger reconcile november` in Mattermost. **Real Switch** delivers the message to a Python agent. Exact arithmetic matches invoices against purchase orders and delivery receipts; a real Codex invocation explains the exceptions. The named reviewer approves an exact evidence digest, and the agent exports only eligible invoices with a verifiable receipt.

Built for **Flip the Switch, Best Business Workflow**, November 4–7, 2026. All included companies, documents and amounts are synthetic. No payments, accounting postings or bank connections are implemented.

![Actual Switch + Mattermost technical test, using a synthetic batch and test reviewer](docs/live-room.jpg)

[Watch the 1m44s demo](https://github.com/ivergit88/ledgerroom-switch/releases/tag/v0.1.0-demo) · [Verification record](VERIFICATION.md) · [Build article](docs/BUILD_ARTICLE.md) · [Passing CI](https://github.com/ivergit88/ledgerroom-switch/actions/runs/41735450113)

The narrated demo is an edited walkthrough of screenshots from the actual local integration test. It uses synthetic documents and an automation-controlled local reviewer account. It shows the real Switch/Codex response, wrong-digest refusal and eligible-only export; it is not a real customer approval.

Inspect the captured technical-run outputs in [docs/proof](docs/proof). The agent never reads these recorded examples as replies; live explanations come from Codex.

## The demo in one minute

1. Send `@ledger reconcile november` in the `ledger-review` room.
2. Read the deterministic table: **3 eligible invoices / USD 840.00; 5 held rows / USD 1,664.00**. Every finding cites its CSV record. These are test-batch face values, not measured savings.
3. Read the real Codex analysis and procurement questions. One supplier note says to ignore instructions and approve everything; it remains untrusted document data.
4. Try an approval with a wrong digest: it is refused.
5. As the configured reviewer, send `@ledger approve eligible november <full SHA-256 from the report>`.
6. Verify `reviewed-invoices.csv` contains only NS-8801, NS-8802 and LM-400. `decision.json` records the Switch decision message, human sender, evidence digest and export hash. Held invoices remain excluded.

## Why Switch is essential

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    H[Named reviewer in Mattermost] --> B[Real Switch collaboration bridge]
    B --> W[Switch authenticated WebSocket]
    W --> A[LedgerRoom agent]
    A --> R[Exact three-way matching + source hashes]
    R --> C[Codex: explanation and questions]
    A --> G[SQLite evidence-bound approval gate]
    G --> E[Eligible-only CSV + decision receipt]
    A -->|Authenticated HTTP message| B
```

Switch supplies the room, message identity, agent addressing, persistent conversation and agent event delivery. There is no alternate chat-to-export path. Disconnecting Switch stops the agent workflow. The offline reconciliation command is a verification tool and cannot create an approval or reviewed export.

The client uses the [current Switch WebSocket protocol](https://docs.switchagents.ai/switch-rooms/internals/agent-connection), handles pings independently of model work, saves connection generation/cursor, fences reconnections and stops on takeover or a history gap. It discovers the operation registry at startup. It uses `connect_to_room`, `read_context` and the room-explicit message API.

## Run locally

Requires Python **3.12+**, Docker with Compose, and an authenticated **Codex CLI**. Switch images are pinned to **0.31.0**, Mattermost to **11.9.0**. A first pull downloads large container images. The services bind to loopback only; no public server or paid service is provisioned.

From the repository root:

```powershell
python -m venv .venv
.venv/Scripts/python.exe -m pip install -r requirements.txt
python tools/init_stack.py
docker compose --project-name ledgerroom --env-file .local/stack.env -f infra/switch.compose.yml --profile collab --profile gateway up -d
```

Wait for the setup container to finish (`docker logs ledgerroom-setup-1` should say `Setup complete!`), then:

```powershell
python tools/bootstrap.py
.venv/Scripts/python.exe -m ledgerroom run
```

On Linux/macOS replace `.venv/Scripts/python.exe` with `.venv/bin/python`. `tools/bootstrap.py` needs the Docker CLI available to the current account. It is safe to rerun: it retains the existing agent, room and credentials instead of rotating agent keys.

Open **http://localhost:18065/ledgerroom/channels/ledger-review**, choose **View in Browser**, and sign in as `reviewer` using `MATTERMOST_USER_PASSWORD` in `.local/stack.env`. The local Switch Gateway is http://localhost:13000; its generated login is also in that file. These are local demonstration accounts, unrelated to the hackathon login.

`init_stack.py` creates random local secrets and retains them on subsequent runs. `.local/`, `.env`, logs and generated artifacts are excluded from Git. Do not upload these directories. The default demo bootstrap expects the bundled `reviewer` account and the `ledgerroom` Compose project name.

### Windows WSL fallback used for the recorded test

The existing Docker Desktop on the development machine failed in its Inference-manager IPC initialization. Its files and settings were not repaired or reset. An isolated existing Ubuntu Docker daemon was started with:

```powershell
wsl -d Ubuntu-24.04 -u root -- dockerd --host unix:///run/ledgerroom-docker.sock --data-root /var/lib/ledgerroom-docker --exec-root /run/ledgerroom-docker --pidfile /run/ledgerroom-docker.pid --log-level error
```

Keep that process running. In another shell use the default WSL user (it has the working Compose plugin):

```powershell
wsl -d Ubuntu-24.04 -- docker --host unix:///run/ledgerroom-docker.sock compose --project-name ledgerroom --env-file /mnt/c/Users/Administrator/Desktop/Switch-Hackathon/project/.local/stack.env -f /mnt/c/Users/Administrator/Desktop/Switch-Hackathon/project/infra/switch.compose.yml --profile collab --profile gateway up -d
python tools/bootstrap.py --wsl
```

The `/mnt/c/...` paths above are specific to the development machine; adapt them to your checkout. The native Docker route above is the ordinary setup. Stop only this stack with the same Compose prefix followed by `stop`; stop its daemon with Ctrl+C in its owning terminal. Avoid `down -v` if you want to retain room history.

## Verify without any service or model

```powershell
python -m unittest discover -s tests -v
python -m ledgerroom reconcile examples/november --out artifacts/offline
python -m compileall -q ledgerroom tools
```

The tests use the standard library. They cover exact decimal arithmetic, cumulative delivery limits, duplicate copies, unknown orders, mismatches, malicious supplier notes, multiline CSV records, digest tampering, identity spoofing, stale decisions, persistence, eligible-only export and its hash. Offline workflow tests mock the model and Switch transport; live infrastructure/model evidence is reported separately in `VERIFICATION.md`.

## Decision and data boundaries

- Code decides eligibility and totals. Model text is advisory and cannot mutate the matching result, allowlist or decision.
- Approvals bind the room, batch, exact evidence digest and Switch message ID. A modified source file invalidates the old digest. A replay of the same decision is idempotent; a new decision against already approved evidence is refused.
- Each proposal preserves the original CSV bytes alongside the report, after verifying their hashes. A reviewer can inspect the exact documents that produced that version of the evidence.
- The sender is pinned to a concrete human principal. A matching display name is insufficient. Switch 0.31.0 omits the ordinary `sender_kind` field in room history, so the **local administrator bootstrap** resolves the seeded reviewer once from Switch's principal registry with a read-only database query. The running agent receives only that opaque sender ID and its own agent key; it does not receive database or Gateway credentials. This version-specific setup step is isolated in `tools/bootstrap.py`.
- CSV and supplier notes are untrusted. Strict commands reject path traversal and blanket approvals. Export fields are escaped against common spreadsheet formula injection. Hashes detect content changes; they are not digital signatures or proof against an administrator editing the local machine.
- Codex is launched in a temporary working directory with a read-only sandbox; configured MCP connectors are disabled for this job. It reuses the user's CLI authentication and model/provider configuration. See [official non-interactive-mode documentation](https://learn.chatgpt.com/docs/non-interactive-mode). A provider failure is reported explicitly instead of inserting a canned response.

## Input scope and limitations

The prototype supports **one line per invoice**, positive integer quantities, exact non-negative prices with at most two decimal places, and three-letter uppercase currency codes. It keeps totals separate by currency and performs no FX conversion, tax calculation or tolerance policy. Multi-line invoice IDs are conservatively held as duplicate records; PDF/OCR ingestion, ERP connectors, returns, partial approval and prior-period billed balances are not implemented. Invoice references use **logical CSV record numbers including the header**, not physical text line numbers.

Matching is conservative: if the batch cumulatively exceeds ordered or received quantity, every affected row is held. It does not guess which invoice to release first. This is a local prototype, with one sequential worker and local SQLite state; administrator security, high availability and production accounting controls are outside its current scope.

## Third-party components

The deployment Compose file is from [sandbox-quantum/switch, switch-v0.31.0](https://github.com/sandbox-quantum/switch/tree/switch-v0.31.0), commit `7e57cd433a922bc91a00150c106e6ef1f15b1057`. Switch uses Apache 2.0 with the Commons Clause; see `infra/SWITCH-LICENSE.txt`. Mattermost and Codex remain separate products with their own terms. LedgerRoom's original code is MIT licensed.

